<mujoco model="pololu_calibrated">
  <!--
    Calibrated hidden plant for the MuJoCo deployment studies (2026-08-10).
    Derived from models/pololu.xml (colleague's original, kept unmodified).

    THIS FILE IS THE HIDDEN GROUND TRUTH. The active-learning pipeline must
    never read it: it only ever sees ROBOTCFG.CFG / robot_config.yaml, exactly
    like the real robot.

    models/pololu_hidden.yaml IS AUTHORITATIVE for every value it lists - the
    motor, wheel geometry, frictions, encoder, IMU and mocap. wmr_simulator's
    build_plant_xml patches them into this file before compiling it, so editing
    an attribute here that the yaml also carries changes nothing. Change it in
    the yaml; the numbers below are kept in step so this file reads true on its
    own.

    The wheel geometry is the real robot's DIRECTLY MEASURED geometry (16 mm
    radius, 84.2 mm wheelbase) rather than anything fitted, because those are
    the two numbers a caliper can reach on the hardware. What identification
    recovers is the *effective* pair below, so the same geometric-to-effective
    gap is present here and on the robot and the two are comparable.

    Changes vs. the original, all measured:

    1. CASTER FRICTION (bug fix). The original declares friction="0 0 0" on the
       caster, intending a frictionless slider. MuJoCo combines friction as the
       ELEMENTWISE MAXIMUM of the two geoms, so it actually ran at the floor's
       mu = 1.0: a skid plate carrying ~27% of the weight. Effects measured:
       open-loop speed 39% low, and a differential wheel command produced almost
       NO yaw (the skid resists rotation on a 41 mm lever). Fixed with an
       explicit <contact><pair>, which overrides the max rule.

    2. CASTER CONTACT SOFTNESS. The caster sat 0.5 mm off the floor and the
       motor's reaction torque pitched the chassis nose-up into it, producing a
       13 N impulse (7x the robot's weight) every ~30 ms and launching the robot
       (8 steps grounded / 7 airborne). solref="-300 -3" on the pair removes it.
       NOT a solver artifact: timestep, integrator, solver iterations, friction
       cone, wheel shape and mass all change essentially nothing.

    3. MOTOR. The original kv=0.5 gives a 78 N-m stall torque on a 0.187 kg
       robot, and armature=0.005 is ~200x the physical reflected inertia
       (rotor 1.5e-8 * 15.25^2 + wheel 4.1e-7). Both were placeholders. Now
       parametrized by three physical quantities:
           omega_max = 230 rad/s   (wheel speed at duty = 1)
           T_stall   = 0.150 N-m   (torque at duty = 1, zero speed)
           tau       = 0.10 s      (free wheel-speed time constant)
       giving  kv = T_stall/omega_max = 6.5217e-4, armature = kv*tau = 6.5217e-5.
       Joint damping is 0 because back-EMF is already the kv term; the original
       damping=0.2 was what made steady-state wheel speed only 0.714*ctrl.

    4. FRICTION mu 1.0/0.7 -> 0.7 on floor and wheels, so a_slip = mu*g =
       6.87 m/s^2 clears the problem yaml's a_max 3.0 and a_max_lateral 4.0.
       At mu = 0.28 the designed trajectories were not executable.

    The duty -> ctrl mapping used by the firmware port is  ctrl = duty * 230.
    A velocity actuator applies torque = kv*(ctrl - qvel), which is algebraically
    a DC motor's torque = (kt/R)*(V - ke*omega), so this is physically founded.

    EMERGENT (not set here, and deliberately different from the nominal yaml):
      effective rolling radius ~15.97 mm   (contact penetration, geometric 16.00)
      effective wheelbase      ~0.0833 m   (scrub, geometric 0.0842)
    Recovering these is the point of the identification benchmark; the current
    measured values live in mujoco_sim.truth and are pinned by its test.

    KNOWN OPEN ITEM: hard cornering (a_lat >~ 4 m/s^2) and fast spin-in-place
    still lose ground contact open loop - realistic oversteer for a rear-caster
    robot. Every test so far was OPEN LOOP, where a differential drive has no
    heading feedback and is unstable by construction. Re-evaluate closed loop.
  -->
  <compiler angle="radian" inertiafromgeom="true"/>

  <option timestep="0.002" integrator="implicit" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>

  <visual>
    <global azimuth="45" elevation="-30" offwidth="1920" offheight="1080"/>
    <headlight ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge"
             rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3" markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8"
             width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="10 10" reflectance="0"/>
    <texture name="wheel_tex" type="2d" builtin="checker"
            rgb1="0.05 0.05 0.05" rgb2="0.9 0.9 0.9"
            width="256" height="256"/>
    <material name="wheel_mat" texture="wheel_tex"
              texrepeat="6 1" texuniform="true" reflectance="0.0"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- friction 1.0 -> 0.7 (see note 4) -->
    <!--
      solref 0.002 1 -> 0.008 2 (2026-08-18). The experiments are run on rolled
      PVC sheet ~5 mm thick: compliant and lossy, not a rigid slab. A near-rigid,
      critically damped contact (0.002 1) let the tripod stance rock. Raising the
      damping ratio is what helps; the time constant must stay SMALL, because the
      binding constraint is penetration - it sets the effective rolling radius,
      which is the thing identification has to recover. Measured penetration:
      0.005 mm at 0.008 2, but 3.1 mm at 0.015 5, which collapsed the effective
      radius 16.0 -> 12.9 mm and the wheelbase 84 -> 68 mm. Check r_eff in
      mujoco_sim.truth before accepting any softer floor. Phenomenological, not
      derived from PVC material data.
    -->
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="0 0 0.05" material="groundplane"
          friction="0.7 0.005 0.0001"
          solref="0.008 2" solimp="0.95 0.99 0.001"/>

    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>

    <body name="chassis" pos="-0.0445 0 0.0161">
      <freejoint name="root"/>
      <camera name="track_cam" mode="track" pos="0 -0.6 0.25" xyaxes="1 0 0 0 0.6 1"/>

      <!--
        MASS AND CoM (2026-08-18). Total was 0.188 kg with the battery pack
        modelled at the rear (x = -30 mm), putting the CoM 11.3 mm behind the
        drive axle. Both were wrong: a 3pi+ 32mm is ~83 g of robot plus ~52 g of
        4x AAA NiMH, and the cells sit SYMMETRICALLY about the chassis, so the
        pack is concentric with the body cylinder rather than hung off the back.

        This matters far more than any contact parameter. A trailing-caster
        differential drive is directionally unstable open loop exactly when the
        CoM is behind the drive axle - the shopping-trolley arrangement - and the
        CoM offset IS the stability margin. Measured, straight line, time until
        heading error exceeds 5 deg, at duty 0.4 / 0.5 / 0.6:

          CoM -15.4 mm (135 g, pack at rear)   3.68 s / 2.26 s / 1.46 s
          CoM -11.3 mm (188 g, as shipped)      stable / 3.02 s / 2.23 s
          CoM  -8.5 mm                          stable / stable / 5.38 s
          CoM  -6.2 mm (this file)              stable / stable / stable

        Open-loop wheel-unload during a gentle arc falls the same way:
        27.8% -> 18.1% -> 23.7% -> 15.7%.

        CAUTION, the trade is forward tipping. There is no front support in this
        model, so the deceleration that lifts the robot over its wheel contacts
        is g * |CoM_x| / h: 6.43 m/s^2 at the old CoM but only 3.37 here, against
        the problem's a_max = 3.0 - a 12% margin. If the real robot has a front
        ball caster, model it; the CoM could then go further forward still (with
        the pack over the axle the arc unload measures 0.0%) at no tipping cost.
      -->
      <geom name="body" type="cylinder"
            size="0.0445 0.01"
            pos="-0.006 0 0.0"
            rgba="1.0 1.0 1.0 1.0"
            mass="0.075"/>

      <geom name="battery_pack" type="box"
            size="0.018 0.020 0.008"
            pos="-0.006 0 0.006"
            rgba="0.2 0.2 0.2 0.0"
            mass="0.052"/>

      <!-- damping 0.2 -> 0, armature 0.005 -> 6.5217e-5, frictionloss 0.02 -> 2e-6 (note 3) -->
      <body name="left_wheel" pos="0.0 0.0421 0.0">
        <joint name="left_wheel_joint" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0"
               damping="0.0" armature="6.5217e-5" frictionloss="2e-6"/>
        <geom name="left_wheel_geom" type="cylinder" size="0.016 0.002"
              rgba="1 1 1 1" euler="1.5708 0 0"
              material="wheel_mat"
              friction="0.7 0.001 0.0001"
              />
        <geom name="left_wheel_marker" type="box"
              size="0.0015 0.0006 0.0015"
              pos="0 0 0.016"
              rgba="1 0 0 1"
              contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      </body>

      <body name="right_wheel" pos="0.0 -0.0421 0.0">
        <joint name="right_wheel_joint" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0"
               damping="0.0" armature="6.5217e-5" frictionloss="2e-6"/>
        <geom name="right_wheel_geom" type="cylinder" size="0.016 0.002"
              rgba="1 1 1 1" euler="1.5708 0 0"
              material="wheel_mat"
              friction="0.7 0.001 0.0001"
              />
          <geom name="right_wheel_marker" type="box"
            size="0.0015 0.0006 0.0015"
            pos="0 0 0.016"
            rgba="1 0 0 1"
            contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      </body>

      <!--
        ROLLING BALL CASTER (2026-08-18). Until now this sphere was rigidly
        fixed to the chassis, i.e. a skid plate, and the low mu=0.05 was what
        kept the skid cheap. That is not the hardware: the 3pi+ carries a ball
        caster that spins freely in its socket. As a skid it dragged sideways at
        ~780 mm/s through every turn, and that parasitic drag is what the old
        6x-oversized motor was silently paying for.

        The free joint here is a BALL joint: the ball rotates in its socket but
        does not translate relative to the chassis, which is what a caster is.
        `frictionloss` is the socket drag - the only thing resisting rotation -
        and it is deliberately tiny. The contact friction is now a REAL friction
        (see friction.caster in the hidden yaml): a ball caster needs enough
        grip to roll rather than skid, which is the opposite of what the old
        skid model wanted.
      -->
      <body name="caster" pos="-0.041 0.0 -0.01">
        <joint name="caster_ball" type="ball" frictionloss="2e-7" damping="0"/>
        <geom name="caster_geom" type="sphere" size="0.0055"
              rgba="0.5 0.5 0.5 1"
              friction="0.4 0.001 0.0001"
              mass="0.002"
              />
      </body>

    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!--
    An explicit pair overrides MuJoCo's elementwise-max friction combination, so
    the caster's own friction is what acts, not the floor's 0.7 imposed on it.
    solref="-300 -10" is explicit stiffness/damping (N/m, N-s/m) and is what
    stops the 13 N hammer impacts the 0.5 mm caster gap otherwise produces.

    The damping was -3 until 2026-08-18, which stopped the impacts but left the
    contact badly UNDERDAMPED: k=300 N/m against the 0.187 kg chassis is
    zeta ~ 0.2, f ~ 6.4 Hz, and the robot never settled. Measured at -3: a 1 deg
    peak-to-peak pitch limit cycle at 5.6 Hz standing still at zero duty, and
    driving circle_medium it pitched to -13 deg at 7.1 Hz with BOTH drive wheels
    under 5% of body weight for 25% of ticks - hopping, not driving. At -10
    (zeta ~ 0.6) the pitch excursion and the tracking error both fall and the
    peak caster force stays ~2x body weight, i.e. the impacts do not come back.

    The rolling friction (4th/5th components) must stay small: that is what lets
    the ball roll. The sliding friction is now high, not low - see the body.
  -->
  <contact>
    <pair geom1="floor" geom2="caster_geom"
          friction="0.4 0.4 0.001 0.0001 0.0001"
          solref="-300 -10"/>
  </contact>

  <actuator>
    <!-- kv = T_stall/omega_max = 0.150/230 = 6.5217e-4; ctrlrange = +-1.2*omega_max -->
    <velocity name="left_motor"  joint="left_wheel_joint"  kv="6.5217e-4" ctrlrange="-276 276"/>
    <velocity name="right_motor" joint="right_wheel_joint" kv="6.5217e-4" ctrlrange="-276 276"/>
  </actuator>

</mujoco>
